#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722146 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1854857e-87eb-5a11-9722-f7e425e95a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722127} {:answer/id #uuid "638f2900-bf66-54f9-9a85-b6424b3e4cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722135} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e36f42-5741-59e2-93f6-85cdf2046d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722140} {:answer/id #uuid "c606330c-7db8-515b-9029-b7a9c0d372b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722143} {:answer/id #uuid "c79fc796-162d-56ce-b2ae-cbfab4ba5823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722135} :answer-field/id #uuid "17515995-3e1c-598c-acc8-1ee723d8f067" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da5d7714-7b4f-50cc-819d-ffdc396bcc8e" :question/problem "![](images/54/545abf42aefd8d15703830dab18ace4d744fef351818e8ee4d9101d2052b26e7.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=\\left(1,-1\\right)$ to $B=\\left(3,1\\right)$, as shown." :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ denote the requested integral. The path can be parameterized by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 t + 1,2 t - 1\\rangle.\n$$\nThe parameter runs from $t=0$ at $A$ to $t=1$ at $B$. Use the signed coordinate derivative $y'(t)=2$.\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=6 t - 1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(6 t - 1\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}12 t - 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}12 t - 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[6 t^{2} - 2 t\\right]_{0}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n The differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$, so an arc-length speed factor is not used."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06af3c32-e74f-51ab-b11a-5ddf6422c314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722151} {:answer/id #uuid "22b1434a-9a3d-5018-95c4-f00e6c84ef79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722155} {:answer/id #uuid "397278ed-a1cd-5aac-b506-315c7fcc1592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722160} {:answer/id #uuid "6076489c-3aed-50aa-8e20-75b23058283d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}} - 64 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722165} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a0e414-cc96-5776-8ed0-1cde24490ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722160} :answer-field/id #uuid "09bf36a8-ca9e-5c8e-8f89-85b9622c98a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4917643a-843b-51be-920e-1a9345c227fd" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/3d/3da9ed3b002a954b70f4aae8a7ee862682c8379688863b87dd49dea16e4eb905.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $16 x^{2} + y^{2}=16$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(\\frac{1}{2},- 2 \\sqrt{3})$ to $(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-2)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 16 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54558b04-4dae-59ef-8864-9f6997f028e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722163} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4de76b-811c-5743-a96b-909a5caef99f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722164} {:answer/id #uuid "63951fe2-6c6a-5111-976b-67bcfc64d9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722166} {:answer/id #uuid "8357452d-bae1-57a7-9436-a36ac84cbfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} - 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722171} {:answer/id #uuid "8b1cf199-a441-5a32-bbde-738cd19a683c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} - 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722171} :answer-field/id #uuid "331f788d-ec63-5044-8a01-d726b48fbe41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "67286858-7abb-5ba2-873d-af5fc384dec1" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/91/91c0bb3ccbb239ce4a24ad76245345da38cd29e495475bd8d306132af414b1eb.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $4 x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=36$, traversed counterclockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(- \\frac{3}{2},\\sqrt{3})$ to $(- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},-1)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated counterclockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{3}=- \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{7 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{6}} - 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722169 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "294fda8a-0188-5465-866d-1334c8a39ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} - 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722156} {:answer/id #uuid "342f3c6e-d1cb-5506-8daf-a73a07e2fc3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} - 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722159} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad170ca-7d6c-52fb-b9a0-44ef87d57987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722168} {:answer/id #uuid "b2984a31-8381-50b4-ac65-d1d255dcfa47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} - 36 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722174} {:answer/id #uuid "f32651c4-ea75-52cd-a9d2-d2d86dd23648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f33ddf97-22b8-5880-a212-0cfffaaada70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6eec3085-13bc-5a40-8a92-15a08014c3ed" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/31/310cd83111af3ba8b0cb57f3ab0268b69eff07244a22f36116ecde25faf73877.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $4 x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=36$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2},-1)$ to $(- \\frac{3}{2},- \\sqrt{3})$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-2}=\\frac{1}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{6}$ and ends at $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le t\\le \\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 18 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)})(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} 54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "170f42d6-b20c-59a8-b5cb-b3fe743fefc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722153} {:answer/id #uuid "1eebcbee-88d2-5288-96f2-0cbcdd6d4b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722154} {:answer/id #uuid "33542dff-a651-5de3-9286-e3c052358206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722158} {:answer/id #uuid "846e7265-3799-5604-afcf-93188c391f0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{7 \\pi}{12}} 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722172} {:answer/id #uuid "c063e007-4942-56c1-b38a-89d783e6ed2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 4 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722175} :answer-field/id #uuid "4475143e-12ce-5b67-8f71-86c7782ceafb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7945137f-f35c-59c8-82e6-c0a5808f9745" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/b5/b5ab08b1bd65b8c691bcbb7e59820f20ff9b6e226d8590efe10c6656e8dab9d5.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=4$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the ellipse from $(1,- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ to $(- \\sqrt{3},- \\frac{1}{2})$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. At the initial endpoint, $\\cos t=\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin t=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus the initial parameter in $[0,2\\pi)$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. Continue the parameter through $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to follow the stated arc. The parameter starts at $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ and ends at $\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 4 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{6}} - 4 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b7ea163-1ad1-5f31-8152-01644b3a435e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} 9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 27 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722183} {:answer/id #uuid "5166aa7c-659a-57a3-8a73-53fd087f445a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}} 9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 27 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722186} {:answer/id #uuid "898840d1-79db-590f-990f-58415f817283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} - 27 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722189} {:answer/id #uuid "d71f645d-39a4-596c-8e01-2c869535d709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} - 27 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722201} {:answer/id #uuid "e5946939-4447-5a76-ae23-f43bda4339de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} 9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722189} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7f415a3-7373-59d5-ac91-7b05def35544" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "988dd1fd-71e6-5ab3-bbad-0358c8936716" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/5e/5ee33fbf6c4e2e19b50e3ba4044892313aa1b1ede922cfa1d6fea668d4169181.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^2+y^2=9$, traversed clockwise through one quarter of the circle from $(-3,0)$ to $(0,3)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $x^2+y^2=9$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated clockwise direction. The parameter starts at $\\pi$ and ends at $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\pi\\le t\\le \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^{2}\\\\&=9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(9 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 27 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} - 27 \\sin^{3}{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cae2e65-5314-55ae-8984-377faaaa178b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} - 16 t e^{- 2 t - 2} - 4 e^{- 2 t - 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722180} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9feeaf-1ff1-57cc-bb7f-51059f3604c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} - 8 t e^{4 t + 1} - 8 e^{4 t + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722181} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1b8b33-42c3-51cc-994e-3653e6758391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} 4 t e^{4 t + 1} + 4 e^{4 t + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722185} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6a6e34-648b-53af-8960-61d4751ddfe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} 8 t e^{4 t + 1} + 8 e^{4 t + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722193} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6f406b-1f89-55c3-9556-8d05bddc419f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} 16 t e^{- 2 t - 2} + 4 e^{- 2 t - 2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722181} :answer-field/id #uuid "996596a5-a75d-5829-82c0-fbebd5927bf2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bf75d8e3-710b-5d12-93b2-329d64c7ffcb" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/f2/f24264686520059e61af6a8d15976af7ac329ccf98ad3f35639e96ac0583d5b7.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment traversed from $(1,-2)$ to $(5,-4)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Interpolate from the initial point to the terminal point. The parameter starts at $0$ and ends at $1$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 t + 1,- 2 t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(- 2 t - 2)e^{(4 t + 1)}\\\\&=- 2 t e^{4 t + 1} - 2 e^{4 t + 1},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 2 t e^{4 t + 1} - 2 e^{4 t + 1})(4)\\\\&=- 8 t e^{4 t + 1} - 8 e^{4 t + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{1} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1} - 8 t e^{4 t + 1} - 8 e^{4 t + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "060af1f4-9528-5e6f-acde-39b3f05622b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} - 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722179} {:answer/id #uuid "346b7d63-4e94-585b-86ff-8ba4d54babf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} - 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 16 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722182} {:answer/id #uuid "427ab8a5-affb-5d5c-94b9-e8c9487f994f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722184} {:answer/id #uuid "ae833856-eb2d-52e3-a7e1-a848a024f019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722194} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ffff86-cb0c-51a4-9398-bf1109017c0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} - 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722194} :answer-field/id #uuid "a35ec79a-4832-5211-b3b0-e330bc34b608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca294e43-18c1-5b9f-81b3-d1598e8f82f2" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/9e/9ec46a661c229864a208a35018ef87451a0bb08ccc9500773dd66cf3c5ab36ab.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x - y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^2+y^2=16$, traversed counterclockwise through one half of the circle from $(0,-4)$ to $(0,4)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The coordinate scales follow from $x^2+y^2=16$. Choose the parametrization below. Increasing the parameter follows the stated counterclockwise direction. The parameter starts at $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ and ends at $\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})-(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\\\&=16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x - y\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} 16 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65ad9bda-543d-5c48-be4d-c1f2cc4d2bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} - 27 t^{2} - 30 t - 9\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722187} {:answer/id #uuid "79bbfb71-f6d8-5a27-97a2-bd80182eddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} 36 t^{2} + 40 t + 12\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722188} {:answer/id #uuid "b81cedc9-97a8-5069-b2e9-83c036cd90f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} 64 t^{2} + 52 t + 12\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722195} {:answer/id #uuid "c570a901-6e02-5db5-89cc-9f25b2627c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} - 64 t^{2} - 52 t - 12\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722197} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f77529-edf9-58e5-8be1-33799ef7ba82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{1} - 36 t^{2} - 40 t - 12\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722188} :answer-field/id #uuid "e56ac8ed-c53f-5828-b394-5c10d98fb1d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d3050808-0475-5213-a16e-9afd16a50b9c" :question/problem "![Oriented path](images/5c/5c2e62972898afdd07d268db0e21a6c0ff3677ddb8b33339b53204498c540cfd.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment traversed from $(2,-1)$ to $(6,-4)$, as shown? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Interpolate from the initial point to the terminal point. The parameter starts at $0$ and ends at $1$, giving the required endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 t + 2,- 3 t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates, then differentiate the first coordinate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(\\mathbf r(t))&=(4 t + 2)+(- 3 t - 1)^{2}\\\\&=9 t^{2} + 10 t + 3,\\\\ x\\prime(t)&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two factors, keeping the signed derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)&=(9 t^{2} + 10 t + 3)(4)\\\\&=36 t^{2} + 40 t + 12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse this density with the oriented parameter limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{1} f(\\mathbf r(t))x\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1} 36 t^{2} + 40 t + 12\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5de5d0-5caf-5b51-be42-fbfdc2ee2576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722698} {:answer/id #uuid "6c66b944-6d37-5298-b9aa-13c4937da887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722706} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba0ac4a-7753-53a0-83a9-f75f96e97e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722709} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb8c6c7-9e7e-5d67-9165-ad1a989c90f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722714} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2f2e5d-0cc4-58f9-8019-cd19aec75591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722706} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb62c57-8422-5f5a-953c-5f4b060c4b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3eed9890-e0cf-5500-9635-3730e4f12854" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent line at $P=(1,0)$ to the curve $xe^{xy}+y^2=x^2+y$." :question/worked-solution "First express the curve as a level curve by moving all terms to the left:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=xe^{xy}+y^2-x^2-y=0.\n$$\n\nThe point is on the curve because $F(1,0)=1+0-1-0=0$. For the first term, use the product rule for the $x$ derivative and the chain rule for both partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F_x(x,y)&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}-2x,\\\\ F_y(x,y)&=x^2e^{xy}+2y-1,\\\\ \\nabla F(1,0)&=\\langle 1+0-2,1+0-1\\rangle\\\\ &=\\langle -1,0\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero even though its second component is zero. It is normal to the tangent line, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\langle -1,0\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle&=0,\\\\ -(x-1)&=0,\\\\ x&=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent is vertical. The zero value of $F_y(1,0)$ does not mean that the tangent is horizontal; the gradient points horizontally, and the tangent is perpendicular to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00664b23-6547-5031-9f1c-c6e5bff94d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y+1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722697} {:answer/id #uuid "517b16cf-9030-5436-9e60-f55ccf6b8548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y+1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722703} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7fce1e-f101-56ff-a9bb-4e1b2a013512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 2,-4\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y+1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722707} {:answer/id #uuid "a42d9533-eafc-5720-bdc0-ab4af87d8ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle -3,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y+1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722710} {:answer/id #uuid "d53da7e9-1eb8-5e06-9cac-0514cf303d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x+1,y-1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722703} :answer-field/id #uuid "680de892-6307-5207-8fa2-313b7b52e917" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "543831c3-6150-54c8-9b95-9d81e46d0447" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=x^2+xy+2y^2$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "For a nonzero gradient, the tangent line to a level curve at $P=(x_0,y_0)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(P)\\cdot\\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle=0.\n$$\n\nFind the partial derivatives and evaluate them at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2x+y,\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=x+4y,\\\\ \\nabla f(1,-1)&=\\langle 2(1)-1,1+4(-1)\\rangle\\\\ &=\\langle 1,-3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is normal to the level curve and to its tangent line. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\langle 1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y+1\\rangle&=0,\\\\ (x-1)-3(y+1)&=0,\\\\ x-3y-4&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $f(1,-1)=2$. The figure shows this level curve, its tangent, and a normal arrow in the gradient direction.\n\n![Level curve through P with its tangent and normal gradient arrow](images/e0/e0d4ac4ba1ada8c2611d8c6a2ea112f008a3891acb81a4312b851589453b131e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45c80835-8864-53ff-845d-18e130ee2c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 2,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722701} {:answer/id #uuid "4cca0cdf-f2a9-5d1c-89e1-0a5c7ebba9e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,-2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722702} {:answer/id #uuid "b2560327-71bd-5ce3-91bd-fb59a5f541f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722711} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdf3c3d-c1db-5377-a016-bb0928ecd221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 2,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722712} {:answer/id #uuid "c36302f8-10ac-5b9d-b0d0-112752cdb857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle -1,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722713}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722701} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edbe11a-9e8e-5d9e-9611-a95fd067d5c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fdc48a54-1298-5c59-a269-60acbe6ce250" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=e^{xy}+x^2-2y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "When taking a partial derivative, hold the other variable constant. The chain rule gives $\\partial(e^{xy})/\\partial x=ye^{xy}$ and $\\partial(e^{xy})/\\partial y=xe^{xy}$. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=ye^{xy}+2x,\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=xe^{xy}-2,\\\\ \\nabla f(1,0)&=\\langle 0\\cdot e^0+2,1\\cdot e^0-2\\rangle\\\\ &=\\langle 2,-1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero, so it is a normal vector to the tangent line. Using the displacement from $P$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,0)\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle&=0,\\\\ \\langle 2,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x-1,y\\rangle&=0,\\\\ 2(x-1)-y&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, the tangent is $2x-y-2=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722747 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35961733-73e4-53f5-a662-8094905b122c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\mathbf i+4\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722750} {:answer/id #uuid "3daf2556-24e3-5f81-b205-de9a900d64da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\mathbf i-4\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722752} {:answer/id #uuid "5d127ce4-84f3-5d25-9879-d3af0e0feeb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722755} {:answer/id #uuid "ed837502-f964-57ff-b2ee-c53aedc99f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722764} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c01808-70c4-52d3-99f6-3a5e4beba191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722764} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ae7b20-4c45-5d9c-8b76-7cab181872dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0f65cb08-eea8-533c-900e-00eaa599906d" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2+2y^2=3.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Set $f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2.$ The point lies on the level curve $f(x,y)=3$ because $1^2+2(1)^2=3.$\n\nThe gradient is normal to the level curve. A nonzero vector perpendicular to it is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives, then evaluate at $(1,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x, & f_y(x,y)&=4y,\\\\\nf_x(1,1)&=2, & f_y(1,1)&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus a tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1)=4\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIndeed, $\\nabla f(1,1)=2\\mathbf i+4\\mathbf j$ is nonzero, and $(2)(4)+(4)(-2)=0.$ Any nonzero scalar multiple of $4\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j$ is also tangent; only one of the listed vectors has this direction.\n\nThe diagram shows the curve and the perpendicular normal and tangent directions at the point.\n\n![Ellipse with perpendicular normal and tangent directions at P](images/cc/cce5568a87e18aa17da2c3a8d86bcb81f735882a23cb8765df17cfa4e113c264.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033e6831-95e2-58b8-bde6-2915a502ff44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722746} {:answer/id #uuid "36ec3219-1cf4-5e53-9f02-5155952aac1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\mathbf i-6\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722751} {:answer/id #uuid "41cfe54c-a914-594f-b165-32ee3aedb7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\mathbf i+6\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722753} {:answer/id #uuid "559dba1f-439f-5bf2-acd1-490d5a9b08f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\mathbf i-6\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722754} {:answer/id #uuid "9278d5ef-bd9f-523e-b651-f13fffa729b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722754} :answer-field/id #uuid "75bc4aae-2bcb-5094-b90a-eef2cc9fedc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b84c02c0-a47d-573d-92db-5f9584443fc5" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2y=xy+y^2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Bring all variable terms to one side:\n\n$$\nx^2y-xy-y^2=0.\n$$\n\nSet $f(x,y)=x^2y-xy-y^2.$ Then the curve is $f(x,y)=0,$ and $f(2,2)=8-4-4=0.$\n\nA tangent vector is $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j.$ Differentiating while holding the other variable constant gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2xy-y,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=x^2-x-2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the given point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,2)&=2(2)(2)-2=6,\\\\\nf_y(2,2)&=2^2-2-2(2)=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(2,2)=-2\\mathbf i-6\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient $6\\mathbf i-2\\mathbf j$ is nonzero, and $(6)(-2)+(-2)(-6)=0,$ so the vector is tangent. Any nonzero scalar multiple is also tangent; the other listed vectors are not perpendicular to this gradient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c66e4c-0a1b-5f08-9760-6b0c56aca2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e^2+e)\\mathbf i+(3e^2+2e)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722748} {:answer/id #uuid "29813f73-67a6-5600-9ec7-dec383af60a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e^2+e)\\mathbf i-(3e^2+2e)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722749} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5bffbb-e301-5599-8eaf-368916b48db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3e^2+2e)\\mathbf i+(e^2+e)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722756} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac6dd99-a171-55dd-a4bc-5f9dc1b138b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^2\\mathbf i-(3e^2+2e)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722759} {:answer/id #uuid "f86c084a-c3eb-588f-a923-9cb28eea656b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(e^2+e)\\mathbf i-(e^2+2e)\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722766}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722749} :answer-field/id #uuid "cacd6fbf-bc3a-546d-80d4-911f000d1107" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ba58e3ff-f338-5746-81c0-49c11eb30791" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xe^{xy}=e^2+2e-ye^x.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Bring all variable terms to the left and set\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=xe^{xy}+ye^x.\n$$\n\nThe curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=e^2+2e.$ The point is on it because $f(1,2)=e^2+2e.$\n\nWe need $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j.$ Apply the product and chain rules before substituting the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}+ye^x,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=x^2e^{xy}+e^x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $f_x,$ differentiating $xe^{xy}$ contributes both $e^{xy}$ and $xy e^{xy};$ in $f_y,$ the chain rule contributes the second factor of $x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,2)&=e^2+2e^2+2e=3e^2+2e,\\\\\nf_y(1,2)&=e^2+e.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA tangent vector is consequently\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,2)=(e^2+e)\\mathbf i-(3e^2+2e)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero since both components are positive. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(1,2)\\cdot\\mathbf t(1,2)\n&=(3e^2+2e)(e^2+e)\\\\\n&\\quad -(e^2+e)(3e^2+2e)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the vector is tangent. Any nonzero scalar multiple is also tangent; none of the other listed vectors has this direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "243a75ee-0e1c-5f3f-9dbe-5bf2f12c0685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722769} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bd5682-5248-57f3-a07a-1ea501b18c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722779} {:answer/id #uuid "d6282734-1302-5c79-9382-917e3b240215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722792} {:answer/id #uuid "e0121d46-cb5f-5cca-86ed-865e85581db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722793} {:answer/id #uuid "f2616a2a-f6cb-5848-a5a3-5a3d96e8b7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722793} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbda239e-d761-56b4-94ad-67ad0f5acf33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2622139d-afde-5730-b86d-9de27df3060b" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $x^2+y=4$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf j=\\langle 0,1\\rangle$?\n\nUse $F(x,y)=x^2+y$ and the tangent-vector formula $\\mathbf t=\\langle F_y,-F_x\\rangle$. Perpendicular vectors have dot product zero." :question/worked-solution "The curve is the level curve $F(x,y)=4$. Its partial derivatives are $F_x=2x$ and $F_y=1$, so a tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y)=\\langle 1,-2x\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFor this vector to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf j$, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot\\mathbf j&=0\\\\\n-2x&=0\\\\\nx&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=0$ into the original curve: $0^2+y=4$, so $y=4$. Thus the point is $(0,4)$. Here $\\mathbf t=\\langle 1,0\\rangle$ is nonzero and has zero dot product with $\\mathbf j$. Every other listed point lies on the curve, but has $x\\ne0$ and hence a nonzero dot product."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7eb466ef-175c-592f-9331-6bab84815b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722776} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f0faa4-add0-5057-b66b-f76b54d3651f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722782} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf15e9c-c7c5-58f3-95eb-0092c1245e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722784} {:answer/id #uuid "c68ad910-1277-5013-a3a7-dae6dbb761be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722787} {:answer/id #uuid "ca38b8cf-71db-5219-b156-6576d379cbb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$, $(2,1)$, and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722788}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722782} :answer-field/id #uuid "e51bb5a5-ac26-594c-8be1-e57a665a30e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4692d5b9-ed92-518a-a93d-1e2a81090928" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $x^2y-xy^2=2$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j$?\n\nChoose the option giving all such points." :question/worked-solution "Set $F(x,y)=x^2y-xy^2$, so the curve is $F(x,y)=2$. Compute the partial derivatives and a tangent vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2xy-y^2, &F_y&=x^2-2xy,\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle x^2-2xy,\\,y^2-2xy\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given vector is $\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j=\\langle 1,-1\\rangle$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot(\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j)&=0\\\\\n(x^2-2xy)-(y^2-2xy)&=0\\\\\nx^2-y^2&=0\\\\\n(x-y)(x+y)&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth branches must be checked against the original curve.\n\nIf $y=x$, then $x^2x-xx^2=0$, which cannot equal $2$. This branch supplies no points.\n\nIf $y=-x$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2(-x)-x(-x)^2&=2\\\\\n-2x^3&=2\\\\\nx^3&=-1\\\\\nx&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $y=1$. At $(-1,1)$ the original expression equals $1-(-1)=2$, and $\\mathbf t=\\langle 3,3\\rangle$ is nonzero with $\\langle 3,3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle 1,-1\\rangle=0$. The answer is $(-1,1)$ only. The other points in the answer choices also lie on the curve. At $(2,1)$, the tangent vector is $\\langle 0,-3\\rangle$, whose dot product with $\\langle 1,-1\\rangle$ is $3$. At $(-1,-2)$, it is $\\langle -3,0\\rangle$, whose dot product is $-3$. Neither point qualifies."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b75e8cec-4205-5a53-816f-a2a00ea72b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722781} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dad9b5-ea55-59c3-b400-b3359fe23bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722786} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec59a3a-3a58-52be-9d6e-0d8d96503e3f" :answer-field/key "$(0,3\\sqrt{2})$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fbbbe69-8db5-56f5-87b6-72d91bd798da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722772} {:answer/id #uuid "9c21c495-12ec-5d2c-89ab-f8cc49d802fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722772} :answer-field/id #uuid "a99027e6-6c8d-587a-941d-3f7a60011c1a" :answer-field/key "$(-3,9)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe87102-5a83-58c9-98e0-907e410febe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722768} {:answer/id #uuid "b89dec9c-62a1-5215-b117-11f6d3f8973d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722768} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa394b79-bdc1-5ff4-81d3-ae739f946af9" :answer-field/key "$(3,-9)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496fc58c-ce9a-5bda-b55d-676ea917b5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722774} {:answer/id #uuid "59c4e886-246f-56b7-a513-2c851fe9379d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722774} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb10e7d3-324e-5400-b63a-4340042e738e" :answer-field/key "$(3,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722789} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c4a5ac3-923f-5d41-adad-da1d497949ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722771} {:answer/id #uuid "fae70af3-49c5-549e-a731-f8ac87de4dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722771} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5ae03aa-ccd1-5a1d-8822-e27bb129ecf6" :answer-field/key "$(-3,-3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd37c74c-d2cc-5c99-95e6-62514e6cffca" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $y^2+2xy=x^2+18$ is a tangent vector perpendicular to $\\mathbf j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $F(x,y)=y^2+2xy-x^2$. The curve is $F(x,y)=18$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2y-2x, &F_y&=2y+2x,\\\\\n\\mathbf t(x,y)&=\\langle 2y+2x,\\,2x-2y\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPerpendicularity to $\\mathbf j$ requires\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf t\\cdot\\mathbf j&=0\\\\\n2x-2y&=0\\\\\ny&=x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $y=x$ into the original curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+2x^2&=x^2+18\\\\\n2x^2&=18\\\\\nx^2&=9\\\\\nx&=3\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y=x$, the points are $(3,3)$ and $(-3,-3)$. The tangent vectors there are $\\langle 12,0\\rangle$ and $\\langle -12,0\\rangle$, both nonzero and perpendicular to $\\mathbf j$. The other listed points also lie on the curve, but fail $y=x$. Select exactly $(3,3)$ and $(-3,-3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52e09bd1-820b-552d-bb4a-36651288251a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723007} {:answer/id #uuid "68175562-7716-5da6-84ae-4fe4466728a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,-2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723010} {:answer/id #uuid "6896e956-597c-58e7-9f91-37fa3bdd1e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723011} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b21cc0-2bdc-5f10-83fb-6a923abf62d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,0\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723021} {:answer/id #uuid "d766826d-7a70-5981-8f5b-69705ce5f732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,4\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723007} :answer-field/id #uuid "b81181b0-ce0e-5c57-9730-3ddbd5f5466e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11fcce50-0fe2-5a68-82e4-3ca724318e94" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- x + y + \\sin{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(-1,1 - \\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 x \\cos{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)} - 1 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=1 - \\cos{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)} \\\\ \\nabla f(-1,1 - \\pi)&=\\langle 1,2\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-1,1 - \\pi)\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle&=0 \\\\ x + 2 y - 1 + 2 \\pi&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 1,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 1,y - 1 + \\pi\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186722999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f1a126-c6c4-502b-bb81-2f4117afd60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 16,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722995} {:answer/id #uuid "811b8c3d-af64-51cf-9396-9a49013f8912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,-8\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723015} {:answer/id #uuid "8b01a991-ae6e-5bc4-ad38-584cb6b153b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,0\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723016} {:answer/id #uuid "9e360434-2103-5954-b7b3-26c2cef44993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 8,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723017} {:answer/id #uuid "f347128f-6098-596f-9cfc-77b0c9e38651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723017} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1a82a2-66fc-547e-b734-b4d4372d9ca4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1610fe1b-1df0-5345-8977-4c4e6d9e79e2" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - 2 x y^{2} - 3 y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(-2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- 4 x - 2 y^{2} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- 4 x y - 3 \\\\ \\nabla f(-2,0)&=\\langle 8,-3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,0)\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle&=0 \\\\ 8 x - 3 y + 16&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 8,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x + 2,y\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180175f0-8773-506b-8b00-8d8c0749b40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 2,3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723000} {:answer/id #uuid "1a87165d-6644-5910-8ae6-e6c699a15409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723001} {:answer/id #uuid "588d4db8-97b0-5154-a824-f43dcee28ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,4\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723009} {:answer/id #uuid "7e8eb4ba-d9b9-5a35-9673-abf4584d07c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723014} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c81500-fb2b-5024-8e41-82d055a7e35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723027}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723014} :answer-field/id #uuid "a678c021-1118-5f75-9554-d3a0acc7f8f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26cb07bf-6d97-557d-869e-11d3463db4a9" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3 \\right)}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{6 x}{3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{4 y}{3 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 3} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle \\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,1)\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle&=0 \\\\ \\frac{3 x}{4} + \\frac{y}{2} - \\frac{5}{4}&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 3,2\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d4705e-9c04-5009-bd31-dfd5bc442a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 4,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722998} {:answer/id #uuid "2c24455f-bbe4-565a-9758-0b5ed4263557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 4,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723004} {:answer/id #uuid "552efae1-602f-58a7-b410-374c8e822f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,0\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723008} {:answer/id #uuid "76775c02-ab74-58be-92b5-d3de3b923549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 2,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723012} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0112ad-8737-53ff-b119-d19d90f6b04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 0,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723024} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bf59fa4-a834-5d4a-94cc-17a2bb462bc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cf119cb2-ad05-56b7-8187-cee41828a3c1" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 2 x + 2 y + \\sin{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through $P=(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Hold the other coordinate constant when taking each partial derivative. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 x \\cos{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)} - 2 \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 - \\cos{\\left(x^{2} - y \\right)} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle 0,1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero, so it is **normal** to the tangent line. Its dot product with the displacement along the line is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,1)\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle&=0 \\\\ y - 1&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying or dividing the whole equation by a nonzero constant preserves the line. Thus the tangent equation is\n\n$$\n\\langle 0,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x - 1,y - 1\\rangle=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d39db4a-5c41-515a-b031-23d70869bc78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x,y - 2\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186722996} {:answer/id #uuid "27d6e692-f80e-5802-b2f6-b8afac11b798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x,y - 2\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723003} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9a4563-d5a4-562e-b2d7-0f75f4da7ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x,y - 2\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723013} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4e801c-489b-50e0-83fa-ce44fbea1c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 5,1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x,y - 2\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723018} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ff6742-69a5-5099-a355-726ec5a736cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 5,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x,y - 2\\rangle=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723003} :answer-field/id #uuid "4904ec20-4a3c-